#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "2f8542a8-1e9e-56f8-9eae-e5b4fb592d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5965" :question/problem "Find the family of surfaces that describe the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y, z) = \\ln(x^2 + 2y^2 - z^2).$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & ={e}^{c} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}+\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{{e}^{c}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{e}^{c}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{e^c} + \\dfrac{y^2}{e^c/2} - \\dfrac{z^2}{e^c} =  1, \\qquad c \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128915 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "6a7316fa-77fe-584f-bd92-a37521371482" :question/math-academy-id "e-5966" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z) = \\sin{xyz}$ that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n  f\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)&= \\sin \\left(\\pi\\cdot 1 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = 1}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡xyz & =1 \\\\ xyz & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2\\pi k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is any integer.\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P\\left(\\pi,1,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ is the surface $xyz= \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2\\pi k,$ where $k$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128916 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "397d8dee-c35f-5908-baa6-002e09dab477" :question/math-academy-id "e-5967" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = x^2+4y^2-z,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c = 0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z = x^2+\\dfrac{y^2}{{1/4}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e44fd69-5864-5b6d-bbce-d61d8ec5d562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4x+y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318542} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d84294-f5d6-51e3-af62-beb6803eceeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318543} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc2a95-e0e3-5052-99d0-7bdbc50f2e90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318544} {:answer/id #uuid "68ba5c70-7eb3-531c-afaa-d24febbf5e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318545} {:answer/id #uuid "91126c2f-bf3b-5c9e-9a4a-ce6aa3a342d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4x-y-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d84294-f5d6-51e3-af62-beb6803eceeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318543} :answer-field/id #uuid "d06afb17-f132-5190-82a7-68f7dd757662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aaa065e5-4d42-53ad-a1bb-ce64f0d24542" :question/math-academy-id "q-129182" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1 & =1 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "557840b2-1c87-5581-b70e-c674e75cdfba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318549} {:answer/id #uuid "9f567d29-a97c-59ba-8814-3e87ae2cf680" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318550} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dfe23a-f83b-5846-96e4-4e0f41451d15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=x+y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318551} {:answer/id #uuid "c18af71a-e98a-597c-8338-7bc77fbabff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{x+y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318552} {:answer/id #uuid "1833156f-bc74-5510-8e51-c5f6d5fde277" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{1}{x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "557840b2-1c87-5581-b70e-c674e75cdfba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=y-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318549} :answer-field/id #uuid "df24c305-ab2b-5f19-9ab0-d5f020dbdd4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe136611-dc75-58d5-ad80-3b8bced294da" :question/math-academy-id "q-129183" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{z}{x-y},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-1.$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{z}{x-y} & =-1 \\\\ z & =y-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=-1$ is the plane $z=y-x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "722e9fe7-52bc-58f5-a99d-17088447b4e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318556} {:answer/id #uuid "fc212aa1-2b52-5605-b31a-ec992818c6f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318557} {:answer/id #uuid "7c128848-f51f-5ecd-9bd3-fb25e4f53f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318558} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ff482a-31ae-5c8b-97fc-d67346a38098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318559} {:answer/id #uuid "172acc8b-bde8-5487-8a12-5f5551793afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "722e9fe7-52bc-58f5-a99d-17088447b4e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318556} :answer-field/id #uuid "a41c64a5-e812-55ef-81a3-7f3e1c56ec62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318555}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ddedfa04-1151-5ff0-acc7-8b052f184f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-129186" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z-1},$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z-1} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z-1 & =0 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=1$ is the paraboloid $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71ce9699-023b-59c5-9569-c6e2b71041af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318563} {:answer/id #uuid "1583e6f2-63e0-58dd-8c64-4dbe81f903ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318564} {:answer/id #uuid "5ac57ccf-f61d-54a4-b93c-dd7da4d1033e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z+2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318565} {:answer/id #uuid "64dbe6fd-20a2-5f77-874d-7020bf40d16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318566} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e4ff0b-7f94-5df5-9bfa-601d35af5673" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z+4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318567}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64dbe6fd-20a2-5f77-874d-7020bf40d16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-y+z-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318566} :answer-field/id #uuid "353c8a90-687c-5f55-816b-7ccce79a0dbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318562}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba672326-c9ff-5430-ad98-64efc5364b6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-129192" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x-y+z-1}$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ={e}^{1-1+1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {e}^{x-y+z-1} & =1 \\\\ x-y+z-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,1)$ is the plane $x-y+z-1=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956e0626-4f96-52ef-b79b-524bf1a85273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318570} {:answer/id #uuid "be6e99ec-8760-530d-9aba-a7779581c8fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318571} {:answer/id #uuid "340d9701-c97a-571e-9bf1-7175e23f28de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318572} {:answer/id #uuid "5b658d57-acec-5180-a95a-c130c6132c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=4{x}^{2}+36{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318573} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab55915-f020-585f-8122-378d7ba3208c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "340d9701-c97a-571e-9bf1-7175e23f28de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318572} :answer-field/id #uuid "211ba566-a416-55d5-9c53-963c66d7ba82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318569}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "597e3f3c-01b1-5b60-85f0-1eda3e7982d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-129194" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point $P(3,1,6).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3,1,6) & =\\frac{{6}^{2}}{{3}^{2}+9({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{18} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}) \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(3,1,6)$ is the elliptic cone\n ${z}^{2}=2{x}^{2}+18{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d3163d-481d-5ee9-b45c-768c96dd0bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318577} {:answer/id #uuid "e019e33c-6c6c-55a7-9bbd-3e8852e82ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318578} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f7ffac-9f86-5028-98e1-1a011e9fc166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $z=x+3y+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318579} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5055ac-cabf-5e49-8ff0-303b17a02c17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperbolic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318580} {:answer/id #uuid "814c62dd-9fda-5f39-87c1-7e08829d1c18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic cones: ${z}^{2}={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03d3163d-481d-5ee9-b45c-768c96dd0bb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of elliptic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318577} :answer-field/id #uuid "e87767bc-2d38-5651-a50a-7158a84da983" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318576}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "852bf822-f29b-5192-aec8-e90f16061ab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-129196" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+z+3?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ -{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}+z+3 & =c \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+c-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we let $c-3=C,$ we get a family of elliptic paraboloids:\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+C,\\,C\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7d42da4-9b70-5a47-a5d2-6e2b651862f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces: $z={e}^{x-2y+{r}^{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318584} {:answer/id #uuid "84fb5007-bb54-548c-92e3-3fbac15ccc13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces: $z={e}^{-x+2y+{r}^{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318585} {:answer/id #uuid "cdacd41f-d844-5aea-85a6-b5614df9f622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $x-2y+z={r}^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318586} {:answer/id #uuid "e9309054-d552-5e4f-8830-1f10182954b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $x-2y+z=-{r}^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318587} {:answer/id #uuid "a6009bb8-b43c-5638-b529-22d1193070dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $x-2y+z=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdacd41f-d844-5aea-85a6-b5614df9f622" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of planes: $x-2y+z={r}^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318586} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9e3ce89-d58d-5eac-b873-20b1b3da36fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b82355ef-f6b0-5644-b274-2b1d50d4f10b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129198" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y+z)?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2y+z) & =c \\\\ x-2y+z & ={e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the exponent ${e}^{c}$ is positive for all $-\\infty <c<\\infty .$ If we let ${e}^{c}={r}^{2},$ we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\nx-2y+z={r}^{2},\\,r\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2548d072-9da1-52b0-9b37-f211d1ae2bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperbolic paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318591} {:answer/id #uuid "19bf0b6e-9f65-5ce3-97d1-f50f926dcb18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of curves: $z={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318592} {:answer/id #uuid "d50bcff7-fb9a-5147-98f6-fa001a9a8ece" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of paraboloids: $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318593} {:answer/id #uuid "400db4cd-98a3-5392-81f3-c03a3abdc0ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of curves: $z={e}^{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}+c$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318594} {:answer/id #uuid "e691a7ce-6608-5118-9675-39a1f5db7951" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of curves: $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+c)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19bf0b6e-9f65-5ce3-97d1-f50f926dcb18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of curves: $z={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318592} :answer-field/id #uuid "39db1192-6cca-5e89-ac58-05369e68c96f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d12435a2-bc4c-5e0c-ae4f-ac947d914e83" :question/math-academy-id "q-129203" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡z & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c \\\\ z & ={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of curves:\n\n$$\nz={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-c},\\,c\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f9679a-77aa-5779-8751-da471ca7f744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 2y + 3z - \\ln 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127053} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0f75cc-9015-553b-8e5d-2cd1d25e55e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = (\\ln 3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127054} {:answer/id #uuid "54b483e6-fc19-5cb7-a406-35b4f3bd48f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + y^{2} + z^{2} = 3^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127055} {:answer/id #uuid "62d88181-cccd-51ca-a3bb-f3dbc441a9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 2y + 3z - 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127056} {:answer/id #uuid "743e62ee-63f0-5e96-837b-48fcba2d0924" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 2y + 3z + \\ln 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62d88181-cccd-51ca-a3bb-f3dbc441a9de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + 2y + 3z - 3 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127056} :answer-field/id #uuid "6dc679e0-a7d6-5713-be8e-680452069640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96466546-d66d-5bfb-9320-7db7a3c30bde" :question/math-academy-id "q-36986" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z)$ that passes through the point $P(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+2(1)+3(0))=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2y+3z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ x+2y+3z & =3 \\\\ x+2y+3z-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(1,1,0)$ is the plane $x+2y+3z-3=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abd64aef-cae7-5f36-bf09-c71b429f26bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318598} {:answer/id #uuid "bd26dcf1-58f9-5b44-ae27-7a90c667017d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318599} {:answer/id #uuid "edd36415-7920-5cf1-9377-c70f903f4908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318600} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea14d98-a14f-533e-af7b-121619326b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318601} {:answer/id #uuid "3135899f-3d87-529c-bdac-3958c0ca17b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318602}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edd36415-7920-5cf1-9377-c70f903f4908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318600} :answer-field/id #uuid "57c88a73-db4d-5bf5-904e-d08eb84dfbbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8672825f-d6f0-5dee-8831-9d4f462607ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-71572" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+z-2,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=-2.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+z-2 & =-2 \\\\ z & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=-2$ is the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5e0a37b-23ad-5e79-a175-378b88640ba4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Level Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e4fe4e4-ed56-5979-ad5e-c45a46888634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318605} {:answer/id #uuid "94f795c5-78ce-522a-a8f7-6729f7c24ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/9}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318606} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c1655c-fbf9-5a01-8590-7fe28a37739b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1/9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318607} {:answer/id #uuid "cffc2cb4-edb9-5761-8ea4-e7b35b895e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318608} {:answer/id #uuid "384756a4-d176-5ddb-b562-298a0a944b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94f795c5-78ce-522a-a8f7-6729f7c24ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/9}+{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318606} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba2919c4-256e-5c44-8e50-e71a6e9dceb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "765d8808-dc7f-55d5-b2be-2ea7a5ecc1b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-71573" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z,$ find the equation of the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ 9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & =9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/9}+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=0$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1/9}+{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56558b26-cace-5491-9917-8b5fef9205c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of spheres centered at the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318612} {:answer/id #uuid "a58cec26-d066-5de7-a50b-dd2d7e0138ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,{e}^{c})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318613} {:answer/id #uuid "aa36eb4c-224a-52e9-8492-ef27157720de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of spheres centered at $(0,0,{e}^{-c})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318614} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0f766b-c78e-52b0-9cef-cc1d2e0375d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by ${z}^{2}+c={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318615} {:answer/id #uuid "7e7da0ca-bee0-5c9d-8fee-3c4b713c97c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by ${z}^{2}+c=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56558b26-cace-5491-9917-8b5fef9205c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of spheres centered at the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318612} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e34b1c8-b84e-5c85-b357-c2bf653de476" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318611}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8f1be0d-70ce-5024-b2a6-192f82740e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71578" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & ={e}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we let ${e}^{c}={r}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surfaces form a family of spheres centered at the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5165085e-ffa9-533a-ab7c-c03304fc1489" :knowledge-point/title "Describing a Level Surface of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cd79d17-842a-5fa3-af0e-ca173253a94b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperboloids of one sheet: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318619} {:answer/id #uuid "7e89b980-cc1c-502e-b7d5-4b9d52e36c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperboloids of one sheet: $cx-2cy+3cz-8=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318620} {:answer/id #uuid "98037671-4d3d-5e59-901d-3ddc5a084545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperbolic paraboloid: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318621} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0f6e1b-1d8d-5ba1-b9b5-d4274a290cfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of spheres: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318622} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2870f3-bd85-5dec-9116-464f03afc8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of ellipsoids: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+{z}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cd79d17-842a-5fa3-af0e-ca173253a94b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A family of hyperboloids of one sheet: $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318619} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4b876f1-d2f6-5dad-973a-274cbe416921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "196e54b1-6877-5611-9d9d-7ecb8d24abce" :question/math-academy-id "q-71582" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the $c$-level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}},$ where $c>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of one sheet:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{c}^{2}}=1,\\,c\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "732c3d24-16a4-58fd-8fd0-a9a6f93badef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318626} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7b662b-d3b6-5e39-8854-fef1dc436af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318627} {:answer/id #uuid "7ed07e63-6b21-5a34-9b4e-f2a23ac17087" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}-\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318628} {:answer/id #uuid "bc209b47-a4f5-5723-a789-d60e6516f63e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318629} {:answer/id #uuid "21c751b9-391b-5c6c-9fb3-0873b76fbbd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "732c3d24-16a4-58fd-8fd0-a9a6f93badef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318626} :answer-field/id #uuid "5329080f-b8a7-5ae0-b1e5-0243f93baa73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c7e17bc-81d9-5d3a-a5a3-3325aa70370c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71596" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2}$ that passes through the point $P(4,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,2,1) & ={4}^{2}+3(2-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}=18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=18.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(4,2,1)$ is the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77be6032-ec22-5561-94c2-be289e343142" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Level Surface of a Function Passing Through a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd7d95cf-c2bf-5940-96c7-2bc4fa3eb331" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318633} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ec7d73-0d7e-5f13-854f-c6fa4e3cf239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318634} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9ad17e-9847-5943-9e1c-3dad79407f4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318635} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f55ce4-2a8f-5362-8787-89e93ac55ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318636} {:answer/id #uuid "341fa4bd-a21a-56f2-ac43-007c571f961b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=2{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8ec7d73-0d7e-5f13-854f-c6fa4e3cf239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318634} :answer-field/id #uuid "f20bdcfa-a054-50c1-a5a6-73f79ea72b4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cee2f9e-e585-5811-8e82-df8782bd57e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71597" :question/problem "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point $P(2,1,22).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,1,22) & =22-4(2)^{2}-6(1)^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ z-4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ z & =4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,1,22)$ is the elliptic paraboloid $z=4{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}.$"}]}
